# Bench ARTEFACTS are never committed — only the harness that produces them.
#
# EVERY DOT-DIRECTORY HERE IS AN ARTEFACT DIRECTORY, AND THAT IS THE RULE
# RATHER THAN A LIST OF THE THREE WE HAPPENED TO KNOW ABOUT.
#
# The list was `/.fixtures/`, `/.out/` and `/.scratch/`, and it was correct
# about all three. It was also a list, so it could only defend the names
# somebody had already met: `.after1/` and `.baseline/` — 26 run reports, the
# same shape as `.out/`'s, written by the same command into a directory nobody
# had named here — were committed with `git status` clean throughout. Neither
# was a new KIND of file. Both were the same artefact under a name the rule did
# not carry, and the next one will be too, because the person copying a report
# aside is naming a moment ("after change 1"), not a category.
#
# So the rule names the SHAPE. A dot-directory at the bench root is scratch by
# construction — the harness itself is the `.bench.ts` files, `fixtures/`,
# `lib/`, `wire/`, `run.ts` and `bench-config.ts`, none of which are hidden.
# Deny by default: a new artefact directory is ignored the day it appears,
# because nobody had to remember to come back here.
/.*/

# WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY STILL ALLOWS. A report kept on purpose for regression
# diffing is a NAMED FILE, committed deliberately, carrying the machine
# conditions the report records — `bench/run.ts --baseline <path>` takes one
# report path, never a directory. That spelling is unaffected by the rule
# above, and it is the only one that makes a kept baseline reviewable: a
# reviewer sees one file with a name that says why it was kept, instead of a
# directory of thirteen whose name says when.
#
# WHAT THE IGNORED DIRECTORIES ARE, for a reader who meets them:
#   .fixtures/  generated scale databases (the `heavy` profile is ~2 GB),
#               reproducible from bench/fixtures/scale.ts with a fixed seed —
#               gigabytes to carry what a 20-second command regenerates
#               byte-identically.
#   .out/       run reports. A report is a measurement of ONE machine at ONE
#               moment; committing it invites comparing numbers taken under
#               different conditions, which is the error this harness exists
#               to prevent.
#   .scratch/   disposable copies of fixture databases plus the pidfiles and
#               logs of the servers bench/wire/load.ts starts. They are
#               mutated by the server under test, so a stale copy would make a
#               later run measure the previous run's writes.
